// * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * //

// WHAT phi IS FOR -- three mechanisms, all of them electrode emission:
//
//   ION-INDUCED (potential/Auger) emission, below ~1 keV:
//       gamma_i ~ 0.016 (0.8 E_ion - 2 phi)      Raizer 1991 sec. 4, after
//                                               Hagstrum. Forbidden when
//                                               0.8 E_ion < 2 phi.
//   THERMIONIC emission (Richardson-Dushman):
//       J = lambda_R A0 T^2 exp(-phi / kT),  A0 = 120.173 A cm^-2 K^-2
//   FIELD emission (Fowler-Nordheim): phi enters the exponent, so the current
//       is exponentially sensitive to it -- a 0.2 eV error is a large error.
//
// phi IS A SURFACE PROPERTY, NOT A BULK ONE, and this is the single most
// important caveat in this file. It varies with:
//
//   * CRYSTAL FACE, by 0.3-1.0 eV on the same metal. W(110) is 5.25 eV while
//     W(111) is 4.47 eV -- a 0.8 eV spread, larger than the difference between
//     most metals here. The values below are POLYCRYSTALLINE averages, which
//     is the right choice for a technical electrode but wrong for a single
//     crystal.
//   * ADSORBATES AND OXIDE. An air-exposed metal is not the metal: oxygen and
//     water adsorption typically RAISE phi by 0.3-1 eV, caesium and other
//     alkalis LOWER it by more. A machined aluminium electrode in room air is
//     an alumina surface, not aluminium.
//   * TEMPERATURE, weakly (~1e-4 eV/K), which is negligible here.
//
// So: THE SPREAD FROM SURFACE STATE IS COMPARABLE TO OR LARGER THAN THE SPREAD
// BETWEEN THESE MATERIALS. Use these values to get the right order of magnitude
// and the right ORDERING, not to predict an absolute emission current. If an
// emission current matters quantitatively to your result, phi is a number to
// measure or fit for YOUR surface, and the case should say so.
//
// FOR INSULATORS, phi IS NOT THE RIGHT QUANTITY and there are no insulator
// entries here. Use the BAND GAP and ELECTRON AFFINITY carried per material in
// etc/materials/dielectrics.
//
// SOURCE for every value unless the entry says otherwise:
//   Michaelson, H. B. (1977) "The work function of the elements and its
//   periodicity", J. Appl. Phys. 48, 4729 -- the standard polycrystalline
//   compilation; cross-checked against CRC Handbook 95th ed., sec. 12.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ELECTRON-INDUCED SECONDARY YIELD: deltaMax and energyDeltaMax
//
// Vaughan's universal curve needs two numbers per material -- the PEAK yield
// and the energy at which it peaks. The curve's SHAPE is material-independent,
// which is what makes electron-induced emission generalizable where a fitted
// gamma_eff(E/N) is not.
//
// Given below ONLY for metals, and only where the compilations agree to better
// than about 20%. Metal yields are reasonably reproducible because the surface
// conducts and does not charge while it is being measured.
//
// DELIBERATELY ABSENT FOR THE INSULATORS in etc/materials/dielectrics. Their
// published spread is enormous -- MgO is quoted from about 4 to 25 depending on
// preparation, Al2O3 from 2 to 9 -- because an insulator CHARGES while it is
// measured, altering the very quantity being measured. That spread is far
// larger than the difference between materials, which is exactly the case G2
// says must not be given a point value. An insulator must be given deltaMax
// explicitly, from a source describing that surface.
//
// SOURCE for every pair below: Seiler, H., J. Appl. Phys. 54 (1983) R1, the
// standard secondary-emission review; cross-checked against Joy, D. C.,
// Scanning 17 (1995) 270.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// --- the common technical electrode metals ---------------------------------
copper
{
    phi              4.65;
    deltaMax         1.3;
    energyDeltaMax   600.0;
    // delta_max 1.28-1.35 across sources.
    // SEE yield from Seiler (1983) R1; see the note above.
    reference        "Michaelson (1977), polycrystalline. The reference cathode of the cold-cathode discharge literature.";
}
aluminium
{
    phi              4.28;
    deltaMax         1.0;
    energyDeltaMax   300.0;
    // Peak near UNITY: an aluminium surface barely multiplies.
    // SEE yield from Seiler (1983) R1; see the note above.
    reference        "Michaelson (1977), polycrystalline. NOTE: an air-exposed aluminium electrode is really alumina -- see etc/materials/dielectrics.";
}
stainlessSteel
{
    phi              4.40;
    deltaMax         1.3;
    energyDeltaMax   400.0;
    // Dominated by the Cr-rich oxide, not the alloy; indicative only.
    // SEE yield from Seiler (1983) R1; see the note above.
    reference        "Not an element and not in Michaelson: a representative value for austenitic (300-series) stainless, whose surface is a Cr-rich oxide. The literature spans 4.3-4.5 eV depending on alloy and finish. Indicative only.";
}
tungsten
{
    phi              4.55;
    deltaMax         1.4;
    energyDeltaMax   650.0;
    // SEE yield from Seiler (1983) R1; see the note above.
    reference        "Michaelson (1977), polycrystalline. Faces span W(111) 4.47 to W(110) 5.25 -- a 0.8 eV spread.";
}
molybdenum
{
    phi              4.60;
    deltaMax         1.25;
    energyDeltaMax   375.0;
    // SEE yield from Seiler (1983) R1; see the note above.
